WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.450
Film Courage: We were talking about 9-to-5
jobs and I wanted to go to something that

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.849
I’ve heard other people talk about and that
I’ve also experienced in the past and that

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:13.240
is you take the job and they know you do other
things outside of there [that employment].

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.210
And they know “Okay, that’s fine” especially
in Los Angeles most people aren’t just that

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.210
job title.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.440
But then they start to get weird with you,
the other workers start to get weird and now

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.670
we have social media and they can kind of
go “Oh?

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:28.740
I see…”

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.899
And it starts to change on you and that’s
when I’ve seen people get pushed out the

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:34.899
door.

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:38.460
Or they start to make you sign things that
they are not making other employees sign like

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.539
“You will not use your phone during work
hours” and you don’t.

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:45.370
But you start to see that the parameters are
different for you for some reason.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:46.410
I don’t know if you’ve experienced that?

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.449
I’ve heard this from other people.

00:00:47.449 --> 00:00:49.771
Rhomeyn Johnson, Actor: Well let me say this.

00:00:49.771 --> 00:00:55.269
I didn’t experience that because I was way
gone by the time that those kind of things

00:00:55.269 --> 00:00:56.269
started happening.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:59.140
But I know people who did experience that.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:04.660
And they would come to me the person who had
experienced that because I had been long gone.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.440
And I would tell them “Well that’s telling
you it’s time to go.”

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.060
Without them having to tell you that.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:17.530
When stuff like that starts happening to somebody
an artist that is working 9-to-5…don’t

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.640
get me wrong.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.320
You have to be able to sustain yourself.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.730
So if you need that work the job.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.920
And you know people say “Should I quit my
job?”

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.930
And I say “Only I you can sustain yourself
without that job.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.330
Otherwise you need that job.”

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:42.060
Because see to do what we do as an artist
this is not the day in age of like 300-400

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.580
years ago when someone would be starving in
a little hamlet somewhere in England.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.940
No…them days are over.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.880
You’ve got to be able to sustain yourself.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.190
If you’re out here on the street and you
don’t have a place to stay, you’re not

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.410
an artist at that point, you’re out here
fighting for your life.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.580
So if you have a job, stay there.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:08.450
You’ve got to try to find a job that is
going to allow you to pursue your craft and

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.640
those are real hard to find.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:11.959
Like I mentioned the Neilson thing.

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:18.040
The Neilson thing was cool because if you
had something to do you would go do your audition

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.750
or job or whatever and might now show up that
day.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.099
As long as you got those [movie] passes out
or whatever, they didn’t have a problem

00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:25.099
with that.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:27.060
So you’ve got to pretty much find a job
like that.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:33.540
That’s why a lot of artists and actors and
people are waiters and servers and all kind

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.409
of stuff like that because those are the kind
of jobs that people understand it all.

00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:41.410
And those are what I call “romantic jobs”
for actors.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:51.170
And those are the few jobs that the industry
higher ups have no problem with an actor being

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.980
or doing.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:58.219
Because when I say romantic I mean in the
sense that all of the top people at one point

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:03.520
or another were waiting tables or a valet.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.432
Those are the kind of jobs that top people
see a young, aspiring actor doing and are

00:03:07.432 --> 00:03:08.432
like “Oh?

00:03:08.432 --> 00:03:09.432
Okay.”

00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:10.829
They remember their experience at that.

00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:11.829
“Oh…wow.

00:03:11.829 --> 00:03:16.420
He’s going to really grow and isn’t that
nice.”

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:22.720
But the industry people are less patient then
people working in Corporate America behind

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.150
a desk somewhere.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.180
Industry people are less patient with that
because they know the difficulty in getting

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.489
out of those jobs.

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:36.810
And see those jobs they always start somebody
off (from what one of my friends told me)

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.450
they always start somebody off with “Oh,
sure…you can take off.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.730
You’ve got an audition, go ahead.”

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:51.669
After a few auditions they start coming down
on them like “Do you really have to go?

00:03:51.669 --> 00:03:54.299
We really need you here to have to do this
or that.”

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:58.169
Then that effects the actor with their agents
and what not.

00:03:58.169 --> 00:04:01.299
Then they have to say “Well I can’t make
it because I have a meeting.”

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:04.319
That’s when the agents and people say “Okay?

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:05.669
What are we going to do?

00:04:05.669 --> 00:04:11.239
I can’t be doing my job here submitting
you speaking up for you to get in and say

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:12.590
“Hey she’s really good.”

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:13.970
Or “He’s tops.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:14.970
You need to see him.”

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.989
And you can’t make it when I set the audition
up.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:22.970
So it’s starts working like that.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:30.810
So it becomes a point if an actor or a singer
or whatever you are in the industry, if you’re

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:36.060
really, really serious about being in this
industry, you’ve got to eventually say “Okay,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.919
I’ve got to leave that.”

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:44.949
And if it means…if you’re making 40, 50,
60 thousand (whatever) or a 100 thousand on

00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:52.720
a corporate job and you really, really aspire
to be in this business you might have to leave

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.630
and go get a job making $12.00 an hour to
be able to do that.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:04.830
And that takes a hell of a lot to do because
if you’re married or have children or something

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.750
it becomes difficult.

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:12.229
Once you have children or something like that
and it doesn’t matter to me if you’re

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:16.680
in the arts or whatever, to me if you have
children, they come first.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.000
The business and all of that comes after that.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.629
No matter how much I love acting or whatever,
if I’ve got a bunch of kids or I’ve got

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:28.520
a child, that child comes first and that’s
the way it should be with anybody.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:33.979
So it’s a real tough thing to do, it’s
a tough nut to crack and you finally have

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:36.940
to just be real with yourself.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:42.009
Like I was saying earlier, you can’t be
with somebody who is not going to let you

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:44.069
be you.

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:47.389
This is the thing that is hard about this
job.

00:05:47.389 --> 00:05:53.690
And we’re not even talking about the actual
rejection which is 99.9 percent of it.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:59.930
And how people they have friends that were
much more talented than me that had to leave

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:02.710
the business because they couldn’t handle
the rejection.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.599
And I’m like “Wow?”

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:10.449
And when I first got in it I had a little
trouble at first and then I had somebody in

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:17.460
the business, my mentor that passed away several
years ago he said “You better get over that

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.659
because you’re going to get rejected a whole
lot more than you’re going to get accepted.”

00:06:22.659 --> 00:06:26.909
I’m like “Wow, is that how it is?”

00:06:26.909 --> 00:06:28.039
And he’s like “Yes.”

00:06:28.039 --> 00:06:30.460
And I’ve learned that’s how it is.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.759
I mean I’m not going to sit here and say
“Oh I’ve mastered that.

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:36.490
I can handle rejection.”

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.400
It gets you at times because you’re human,
you are real, you’re a human being.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.199
And sometimes it hits you, sometimes it hits
you not only because “Damn that job would

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:47.350
have paid me a lot of money.”

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.380
Other times it hits you because “Wow?

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.979
I thought I was really good and that’s the
part I want to play.”

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.550
Not even considering any money.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:07:00.740
It’s just the fact that your artistic thing
“I could really get into that role.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:01.860
That’s a challenging role.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.680
That would be really good for me to do that.”

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.300
And then you don’t get it.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:11.539
So it hits you in a lot of ways, so you’ve
just got to eventually…and it’s a thing

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:14.490
I keep going back to, you’ve got to be true
to yourself.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:16.319
You’ve got to be true to yourself.

00:07:16.319 --> 00:07:19.610
You’ve got to be true to yourself not only
as far as a job, you’ve got to be true to

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:24.000
yourself as far as can I handle what I’m
doing?

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.460
Can I handle this?

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.389
Can I handle the fact that I’m not going
to have a lot of money at a certain point?

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:32.870
Or I’ not going to get a job and I’m going
to be depressed about sh*t.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.030
Can I handle this?

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:40.050
And you just finally have to say “Okay,
yes I can" or “No, I can’t.”

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:43.960
All these friends that I had when they left
the business or what not when I see them or

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:50.740
I go home for the Holidays or on Facebook
or with some people we stay in touch and talk,

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:55.800
I don’t hold anything against them as far
as not being an actor “You’re not an actor

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.800
anymore?

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:57.990
I can’t relate to you.”

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:58.990
No!

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:01.229
These are friends people that I love and care
for.

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:02.759
And I tell people, I say “Hey!”

00:08:02.759 --> 00:08:07.130
And what I aways do is, most of my friends
who have to leave the business and go out

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:10.370
of here, most of them go home and don’t
have any money and can’t really get home.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:16.259
I provide everybody a way to get home, I’ve
sent many friends home.

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:18.569
Not on the Greyhound because I wound’t do
that to them.

00:08:18.569 --> 00:08:27.129
Because if you’re coming from LA to Chicago
or Detroit or New York that’s a hell of

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:28.129
a long ride.

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:29.129
So I fly them home.

00:08:29.129 --> 00:08:33.159
I mean I’ve sent people home with one-way
tickets and I have people say “You’re

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:34.159
always sending somebody home?”

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:35.750
And I say “I do that.”

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.790
Because if they’re broke or whatever and
they can’t get out of here and need to go,

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:40.810
I help them.

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:44.080
I had a friend who left (she drove).

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.000
I said “Are you going to make it in that
car?”

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.500
She said “I’m going to make it!”

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.770
And she said “Well I’m going to need about
200 dollars.”

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.959
I said “Okay.”

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:53.100
I gave her 200 dollars.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.810
And other people said “You gave her 200
dollars!”

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.540
I said “Yeah, she’s got to go home.”

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.070
She’s like Dorothy, she’s got to go home.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.110
She’s clickin’ them heels to get the hell
out of here.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:04.110
Go!

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.839
I’m going to help you get out of here.

00:09:07.839 --> 00:09:13.060
Because that’s what you have to do and people
thank me and I say “Oh man, I would hope

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.170
you’d do it for me if that were the case?”

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.839
I mean I’m sending two or three people home
a year.

